\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Every ratio $r$ below is the paired width ratio defined in \cref{sec:experiments}, and ``label saving'' is $1-r^2$. ``PPI++'' is the package's; the ``strongest valid baseline'' is the narrower of the package's and the unclipped PPI++ among those with valid coverage in the cell (the narrower of the two if neither is valid). Coverage is nominal 95\%, from at least 2000 repetitions per cell (5000 for $n\le100$); ``the selected interval'' is the headline arm of \cref{sec:experiments}.

\paragraph{Real data: no interval we build is more than about 1\% shorter than the strongest PPI++.}
At the reference $n$ the selected interval has $r$ between $0.994$ and $1.008$ against the strongest valid baseline on the nine datasets, and all nine are classified \emph{no gain} (\cref{fig:ladder}, \cref{tab:main}). Against the package's PPI++ the range is $0.971$ to $1.007$ and eight are no gain; \texttt{gene\_expression} ($r=0.971$, label saving $5.7\%$) is undecided, for the reason given below. In measured widths the estimated floor is between $0.967$ and $1.010$ times the package's PPI++ interval ($0.967$ on \texttt{gene\_expression}, within $1\%$ of it on the other eight). In the model classes we fit, then, no interval built from the same labels, unlabeled records and $f$ is more than a few percent shorter than PPI++ at that $n$. This is an estimate within a class, not a bound: seven cross-fitted classes (splines, isotonic, strata means, boosting) change $\sigma^2_{\mathrm{res}}$ by less than $0.2\%$ on every dataset, but a richer class could find more. Over the $51$ dataset--$n$ cells ($n$ from 30 to 3000), against the package $38$ are no gain, $12$ undecided and one is a gain (\texttt{gene\_expression}, $n=100$, $r=0.930$, saving $13\%$); against the strongest valid baseline $39$ are no gain, $12$ undecided and none is a gain. At the reference $n$ the baseline itself undercovers on three datasets (\texttt{alphafold}, \texttt{census\_income}, \texttt{forest}; $r=1.000$, $1.008$, $1.007$ against the narrower of the two), so those three no-gain cells are read with the validity caveat. The labels-only interval is $1.15$ to $2.9$ times as wide as PPI++, so the use of $f$ and the step to PPI++ matter far more than what remains.

